in my dashboard.component.html

answer:
To address the `ReferenceError: window is not defined` issue in your Angular 17 SSR application with ApexCharts, you can use the `afterNextRender()` lifecycle hook to ensure that ApexCharts initialization runs only on the client side. Here’s how you can implement it:

```typescript
import { Component, ViewChild, afterNextRender } from '@angular/core';
import ApexCharts from 'apexcharts';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-dashboard',
  templateUrl: './dashboard.component.html'
})
export class DashboardComponent {
  @ViewChild('chart') chart!: ElementRef;

  constructor() {}

  afterNextRender() {
    const element = this.chart.nativeElement;
    if (typeof window !== 'undefined' && element) { // Ensure we are in the browser
      new ApexCharts(element, {
        series: [
          { name: 'STOCK ABC', data: [/* your data */] }
        ],
        chart: { type: 'line' }, // Add other options as needed
        xaxis: { categories: ['Jan', 'Feb', 'Mar'] } // Example categories
      }).render();
    }
  }

  ngOnInit() {
    afterNextRender(this.afterNextRender, { phase: AfterRenderPhase.Read });
  }
}
```

This approach ensures that ApexCharts initialization only occurs in the browser environment. The `afterNextRender` hook is used to run your custom logic after Angular has finished rendering the component, making sure it runs on the client side where `window` is defined.

Make sure to replace placeholder data with actual data from your application.

