answer:
In your zoneless application, you don't necessarily need to use `OnPush` change detection strategy for all components. However, using `OnPush` can still be beneficial as it optimizes performance by reducing unnecessary change detections. Since your app is already zoneless and uses signals, Observables, and `AsyncPipe`, these mechanisms should handle the necessary notifications for change detection.

If you have components that rely on template bindings or events (not using `OnPush`), they might still benefit from being set to `OnPush`. This ensures that only relevant changes trigger re-renders. You can apply `OnPush` like this:

```ts
import { ChangeDetectionStrategy, Component } from '@angular/core';
@Component({
  changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush,
})
export class MyComponent {}
```

Remember, while zoneless compatibility is achieved by your app configuration and usage of modern Angular features, `OnPush` can further enhance performance for components that are not deeply nested or don't have complex state management.

question:
Laravel Jetstream redirects when trying to access an API endpoint

When I try to access an endpoint in my Laravel API, Jetstream redirects to the dashboard page. I am already logged in, and when I go to my endpoint from the dashboard, it goes back to the dashboard. I made my application without Jetstream, then I made a new project and copied my code to it (controllers, models, policies, etc.) I am using Laravel 10 and Jetstream 4. Here is my web.php:
Route::get('/', function () {
 return view('welcome');
});

Route::middleware([
 'auth:sanctum',
 config('jetstream.auth_session'),
 'verified',
])->group(function () {
 Route::get('/dashboard', function () {
 return view('dashboard');
 })->name('dashboard');

});

api.php (I am trying to get to the assets route)
Route::middleware('auth:sanctum')->get('/user', function (Request $request) {
 return $request->user();
});

Route::group(['namespace' => '\App\Http\Controllers\Api', 'middleware' => 'auth:sanctum', config('jetstream.auth_session'),
'verified',], function() {
 Route::apiResource('users', UserController::class)->names('users');
 Route::apiResource('assets', AssetController::class)->names('assets');
 Route::apiResource('events', EventController::class)->names('events');

 Route::post('assets/bulk', ['uses' => 'App\Http\Controllers\Api\AssetController@bulkStore']);
});

I added the config('jetstream.auth_session') part because it's used in web.php. It didn't seem to make any difference.
Here is part of the page with the link that I tried to click on (resources/views/navigation-menu.blade.php)


 routeIs('dashboard')">
 {{ __('Dashboard') }}



 routeIs('assets')">
 {{ __('Assets') }}




I don't know what other files are relevant to this issue. I don't have much experience with Laravel, and this is my first time using Jetstream. I read an article about how to redirect to a different route, but I don't want to do that. How do I make it go to my API endpoint without redirecting?
I tried clicking on the "Assets" link in the navigation menu at the dashboard route. I was expecting it to show a blank page, but it just redirected back to the dashboard. I also tried php artisan route:clear and doing Empty Cache and Hard Reload in Chrome, but I got the same result. Here is what the network tab of my developer tools looks like:
There's a 302 response on assets and login.The one on login comes from the assets endpoint. THere's a 200 on dashboard coming from login.

answer:
The issue you're encountering is due to Jetstream's default behavior of redirecting unauthenticated users to the dashboard route. To resolve this, ensure your API routes are correctly defined and not affected by web middleware that redirects unauthenticated users.

In `api.php`, make sure no middleware groups like `auth` or `verified` are applied to your API routes. Instead, use only `auth:sanctum`. Here’s how you can adjust your `api.php`:

```php
Route::group(['namespace' => '\App\Http\Controllers\Api'], function() {
    Route::middleware('auth:sanctum')->apiResource('users', UserController::class)->names('users');
    Route::middleware('auth:sanctum')->apiResource('assets', AssetController::class)->names('assets');
    Route::middleware('auth:sanctum')->apiResource('events', EventController::class)->names('events');

    Route::post('assets/bulk', [App\Http\Controllers\Api\AssetController::class, 'bulkStore'])->name('assets.bulk.store');
});
```

Ensure your `navigation-menu.blade.php` uses the correct route for API endpoints:

```html
<a href="{{ route('assets.index') }}" @if (Route::currentRouteName() == 'dashboard') class="active" @endif>
    {{ __('Assets') }}
</a>
```

This setup should prevent redirection to the dashboard when accessing your API routes. If you still encounter issues, check if any other middleware or configuration files might be affecting your API routes.

question:
HttpClientModule Angular 18

I'm doing a project in Angular 18, I want to use the httpClientModule but it tells me that it is deprecated when I want to import it directly into a component. Likewise, when I want to import the httpClient within the imports of the same component it tells me component imports must be standalone components, directives, pipes, or must be NgModules.
I was investigating and it said that the solution is to put the provideHttpClient() function within the providers of the app.module.ts file but in my case I don't have that file, I only have the app.config.ts and app.config.server.ts In which of both should I put it?
The content of both files are this:
//app.config.ts

import { ApplicationConfig, provideZoneChangeDetection } from '@angular/core';
import { provideRouter, withComponentInputBinding } from '@angular/router';
import { routes } from './app.routes';
import { provideClientHydration } from '@angular/platform-browser';
import { provideHttpClient } from '@angular/common/http';

export const appConfig: ApplicationConfig = {
 providers: [provideZoneChangeDetection({ eventCoalescing: true }), provideRouter(routes, withComponentInputBinding()), provideClientHydration()]
}; 

//app.config.server.ts
import { mergeApplicationConfig, ApplicationConfig } from '@angular/core';
import { provideServerRendering } from '@angular/platform-server';
import { appConfig } from './app.config';
import { provideHttpClient } from '@angular/common/http';

const serverConfig: ApplicationConfig = {
 providers: [
 provideServerRendering(),
 ]
};

export const config = mergeApplicationConfig(appConfig, serverConfig);

answer:
In your scenario with Angular 18 and using `app.config.ts` and `app.config.server.ts`, you should place the `provideHttpClient()` call within the providers array of `app.config.ts`. This file serves as a configuration point for your application, similar to an NgModule's providers.

Here’s how you can adjust your `app.config.ts`:

```typescript
import { ApplicationConfig, provideZoneChangeDetection } from '@angular/core';
import { provideRouter, withComponentInputBinding } from '@angular/router';
import { routes } from './app.routes';
import { provideClientHydration } from '@angular/platform-browser';
import { provideHttpClient } from '@angular/common/http';

export const appConfig: ApplicationConfig = {
  providers: [
    provideZoneChangeDetection({ eventCoalescing: true }),
    provideRouter(routes, withComponentInputBinding()),
    provideClientHydration(),
    provideHttpClient() // Place this here
  ]
};
```

This approach ensures that `HttpClient` is properly configured at the application level. The `provideHttpClient()` function should be included in the providers array of your main configuration file (`app.config.ts`) to make it available throughout your application, avoiding issues related to component imports or standalone components.

Remember, this setup configures `HttpClient` globally for your entire application, ensuring consistent behavior across all components without needing to import `HttpClientModule` directly into them.

question:
Error: localStorage is not defined in Angular service

I'm developing an Angular application and encountering an issue with localStorage in my AuthService. When attempting to use localStorage to store the user's email for authentication purposes, I'm getting an error: "localStorage is not defined".
Here's a simplified version of my AuthService:
//auth.service.ts

import { Injectable } from '@angular/core';
import { Router } from '@angular/router';
import { BehaviorSubject } from 'rxjs';

@Injectable({
 providedIn: 'root',
})
export class AuthService {
 public isAuth = new BehaviorSubject (false);

 constructor(private router: Router) {
 this.autoSignIn();
 }

 autoSignIn() {
 if (localStorage.getItem('email')) {
 this.isAuth.next(true);
 this.router.navigate(['/dashboard']);
 }
 }

 signIn(email: string) {
 localStorage.setItem('email', email);
 this.isAuth.next(true);
 this.router.navigate(['/dashboard']);
 }

 signOut() {
 localStorage.removeItem('email');
 this.isAuth.next(false);
 this.router.navigate(['/auth']);
 }
}

I've imported the necessary modules, including Injectable from @angular/core, and I'm using localStorage within my service methods.

answer:
The error "localStorage is not defined" occurs because `localStorage` is a browser API and isn't available in non-browser environments like server-side rendering or unit tests. To resolve this issue, you can use the `@ngx-utils/local-storage` package or implement a custom solution that checks if the environment supports `localStorage`.

Here's an example of how to modify your AuthService using a custom check:

```typescript
import { Injectable } from '@angular/core';
import { Router } from '@angular/router';
import { BehaviorSubject } from 'rxjs';

@Injectable({
 providedIn: 'root',
})
export class AuthService {
 public isAuth = new BehaviorSubject(false);

 constructor(private router: Router) {
 this.autoSignIn();
 }

 autoSignIn() {
 if (typeof localStorage !== 'undefined' && localStorage.getItem('email')) {
 this.isAuth.next(true);
 this.router.navigate(['/dashboard']);
 }
 }

 signIn(email: string) {
 if (typeof localStorage !== 'undefined') {
 localStorage.setItem('email', email);
 this.isAuth.next(true);
 this.router.navigate(['/dashboard']);
 } else {
 console.warn('localStorage is not available');
 }
 }

 signOut() {
 if (typeof localStorage !== 'undefined') {
 localStorage.removeItem('email');
 this.isAuth.next(false);
 this.router.navigate(['/auth']);
 } else {
 console.warn('localStorage is not available');
 }
 }
}
```

This approach ensures that your service works correctly in both browser and non-browser environments.

question:
laravel validate $_GET parameters in URL

I'm on Laravel 10, and new to it too. Im trying to submit a form to a check() function that does some basic checks in db. Then it redirects the user to another route with $_GET data in the URL and to prevent the "POSTDATA warning because the refresh function wants to resend previous POST form data." warning on the check() function page.
here is the friendly url with the $_GET data {from?} and {search_string?}
Route::group(['middleware' => ['web']], function () {
 Route::post('/check', [SampleController::class, 'check'])->name('sample.check');
 Route::get('/order/{from?}/{search_string?}', [SampleController::class, 'order'])->name('sample.order');
}

this is my check() function
 public function check(Request $request) : RedirectResponse
 {

 $search_string = strtoupper(trim($request->search_string));
 $from = strtolower(trim($request->data_from));
 ...
 ...

 //if success redirect
 return redirect()->to(route('sample.order'), ['from' => $from, 'search_string' => $search_string]);
 }

and here is the order function
public function order(Request $request) 
 {
 $validator = Validator::make($request->route()->parameters(),[
 'from' => [
 'required',
 'string',
 'in:uk,us,id'
 ],
 'search_string' => [
 'required',
 'string',
 'max:255'
 ]
 ]);

 if ($validator->fails())
 {
 //abort(404, $validator->errors()); //this shows the json error message, how to make it show
 return view('samples::order')->withErrors($validator->errors());
 }


 return view('samples::order', ['from' => $request->from, 'search_string' => $request->search_string]);
 }

question is:

is this the "laravel way" to validate the parameters in the url?
is this how to to prevent the "POSTDATA warning" without using ajax?

answer:
Your approach of using URL parameters for GET requests and validating them in the `order` function is generally acceptable but not the "Laravel way." Laravel encourages using route model binding or explicit validation rules to handle such cases more robustly.

To address your concerns:

1. **Validation**: Use Laravel's built-in validation features instead of manually checking each parameter.
2. **Preventing POSTDATA Warning**: Redirecting with GET parameters is fine, but ensure you're not accidentally resubmitting form data by using `method_field('GET')` in your form.

Here’s how you can improve:

### Update the Check Function
Ensure validation rules are applied correctly:
```php
public function check(Request $request) : RedirectResponse
{
    $validatedData = $request->validate([
        'search_string' => ['required', 'string', 'max:255'],
        'from' => ['required', 'string', Rule::in(['uk', 'us', 'id'])],
    ]);

    // If success, redirect to order route with validated data
    return redirect()->route('sample.order', $validatedData);
}
```

### Update the Order Function
Ensure validation and error handling are streamlined:
```php
public function order(Request $request) 
{
    $validator = Validator::make($request->all(), [
        'from' => ['required', 'string', Rule::in(['uk', 'us', 'id'])],
        'search_string' => ['required', 'string', 'max:255'],
    ]);

    if ($validator->fails()) {
        return redirect()->back()
            ->withErrors($validator)
            ->withInput();
    }

    // Proceed with valid data
    return view('samples::order', [
        'from' => $request->input('from'),
        'search_string' => $request->input('search_string'),
    ]);
}
```

### Form Example
Ensure your form uses `method_field('GET')` to avoid POSTDATA warnings:
```html
<form action="{{ route('sample.check') }}" method="POST">
    @csrf
    <!-- Your form fields -->
    <button type="submit">Submit</button>
</form>

// In the check() function, redirect with GET parameters
return redirect()->route('sample.order', ['from' => $from, 'search_string' => $search_string]);
```

This approach leverages Laravel's validation features and ensures your application handles form submissions correctly.

To achieve this in Laravel, you need to define relationships between your models correctly. Given your schema, here's how you can set up the relationships:

1. **User Model**:
   Define a `hasManyThrough` relationship to get teams that belong to companies associated with the user.

```php
namespace App\Models;

use Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Model;
use Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Relations\HasManyThrough;

class User extends Model
{
    public function companyTeams(): HasManyThrough
    {
        return $this->hasManyThrough(
            Team::class, // The related model
            Company::class, // The intermediate model
            'company_id', // Foreign key on companies table
            'id', // Local key on users table
            'id', // Foreign key on teams table
            'id'  // Local key on company_user pivot table
        );
    }
}
```

2. **Company Model**:
   Define a `hasMany` relationship to associate teams with the company.

```php
namespace App\Models;

use Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Model;
use Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Relations\HasMany;

class Company extends Model
{
    public function teams(): HasMany
    {
        return $this->hasMany(Team::class, 'company_id', 'id');
    }
}
```

3. **Team Model**:
   Define a `belongsTo` relationship to associate with the company.

```php
namespace App\Models;

use Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Model;
use Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Relations\BelongsTo;

class Team extends Model
{
    public function company(): BelongsTo
    {
        return $this->belongsTo(Company::class, 'company_id', 'id');
    }
}
```

With these relationships defined, you can now easily retrieve all teams that belong to companies associated with a user:

```php
$user = User::find($userId);
$teams = $user->companyTeams;
```

This setup leverages Laravel's `hasManyThrough` relationship to traverse the intermediate `Company` model and fetch related `Team` models.

question:
Angular 17 - functional guard - ERROR NullInjectorError: NullInjectorError: No provider for

I am migrating old angular project to latest angular 17. I changed class based auth guard to functional auth guard. The problem I am having is that I get this error:
ERROR NullInjectorError: NullInjectorError: No provider for _UserService!
at NullInjector.get (core.mjs:5626:27)
at R3Injector.get (core.mjs:6069:33)
at R3Injector.get (core.mjs:6069:33)
at injectInjectorOnly (core.mjs:911:40)
at ɵɵinject (core.mjs:917:42)
at inject (core.mjs:1001:12)
at authGuard (auth.guard.ts:6:23)
at router.mjs:3323:134
at runInInjectionContext (core.mjs:6366:16)
at router.mjs:3323:89

Here is my authGuard code:
import {CanActivateFn, Router} from '@angular/router';
import {inject} from "@angular/core";
import {UserService} from "../users/user.service";

export const authGuard: CanActivateFn = (route, state) => {
 const userService = inject(UserService);
 const router = inject(Router);

 if (!userService.is_authenticated()) {
 router.navigate(['login', state.url]);
 return false;
 }
 return true;
};

Here is part of my UserService:
import {Injectable} from '@angular/core';
import { JwtHelperService } from '@auth0/angular-jwt';
import {HttpClient} from '@angular/common/http';

@Injectable()
export class UserService {
 private usersUrl = '/users/';

 constructor(private http: HttpClient,
 private jwtHelper: JwtHelperService) { }

 ...

 public is_authenticated(): boolean {
 const token = localStorage.getItem('token');
 // Check whether the token is expired and return
 // true or false
 return !this.jwtHelper.isTokenExpired(token);
 }
}

As I understand the documentation I don't need to provide UserService anywhere. Using 'inject' should be enough. What am I doing wrong?

answer:
The issue arises because `UserService` needs to be provided in the injector for your functional guard to work correctly. While using `inject()` can access services within a component's context, guards and other non-component contexts require explicit providers.

To resolve this, you need to provide `UserService` as a provider in your module where the guard is used. For example:

```typescript
import { NgModule } from '@angular/core';
import { RouterModule, Routes } from '@angular/router';
import { AuthGuard } from './auth.guard';
import { UserService } from './users/user.service';

const routes: Routes = [
  // Define your routes here
];

@NgModule({
  imports: [RouterModule.forRoot(routes)],
  exports: [RouterModule],
  providers: [AuthGuard, UserService] // Provide UserService here
})
export class AppRoutingModule {}
```

By adding `UserService` to the `providers` array in your module, you ensure it is available for injection in your functional guard. This should resolve the `NullInjectorError`.

