  });
}
```

This setup ensures both Swipers are initialized only once when the component is first loaded or reloaded after a route change. Adjust `swiperParams` and `swiperParams1` to fit your specific configuration needs.

question:
How to return a 404 status and view from Laravel middleware

I'm building an admin section for a Laravel 10 app using the boilerplate Breeze routes and templates (for now) and I have a group of prefixed routes that I want to return a 404 error for in the following cases:

there is NO signed in user
if there IS a signed in user, they are not an admin

In other words, the ONLY time the prefixed routes should produce anything substantial is if the authenticated user is an admin. Otherwise, I want it to appear as the routes don't even exist.
I am at my wits end trying to return the 404 error view and status code from the middleware. I have been tinkering with the code and reading documentation for HOURS now to no avail, and yet I get the feeling the answer is right in front of me but I'm missing something.
This is code I have in the web.php routes file:
Route::prefix('admin')->middleware(['admin'])->group(function() {
 Route::get('/', [AdminDashboardController::class, 'index'])->name('admin');
});

And here's what I have in the handle() function for the admin middleware:
public function handle(Request $request, Closure $next): Response
{
 if ($request->user() === null || $request->user()->role !== 'admin') {
 return response('This page cannot be found', 404, []);
 }

 return $next($request);
}

This code works just fine. BUT, I want to return the 404 page and HTTP 404 status code rather than a plain text page with the response message. If I try to return anything EXCEPT the above it just seems to skip right on to the next bit of middleware. As in, it attempts to render the Breeze dashboard component as if the user was signed in, even though the user isn't signed in.
Could anybody please guide me on how I can return the 404 error page and status code in place of the current response above?

answer:
To return a custom 404 view along with the HTTP 404 status code from your middleware, you need to use Laravel's `response()->view()` method instead of returning plain text. Here’s how you can modify your `handle` function:

```php
public function handle(Request $request, Closure $next): Response
{
    if ($request->user() === null || $request->user()->role !== 'admin') {
        return response()->view('errors.404', [], 404);
    }

    return $next($request);
}
```

This code checks if the user is not authenticated or not an admin. If either condition is true, it returns a view for the 404 error page with the appropriate HTTP status code.

Ensure you have a `resources/views/errors/404.blade.php` file to define your custom 404 view. You can publish Laravel’s default error pages using:

```shell
php artisan vendor:publish --tag=laravel-errors
```

This will give you a starting point for customizing the 404 page according to your needs.

By returning `response()->view('errors.404', [], 404)`, Laravel will render the specified view and set the HTTP status code to 404, effectively showing your custom 404 error page when the conditions are not met.

question:
How to publish service provider to the application in laravel 11

I want to add service provider in laravel 11, but i am not sure how to add it using laravel 11. As previous version of laravel, it is added in config/app.php file, but in laravel 11 it needs to be added in packageServiceProvider file within providers folder.
Below is my code, please tell me if i am wrong somewhere..
 Anand\LaravelPaytmWallet\PaytmWalletServiceProvider::class,
 ];

 /**
 * Register services.
 */
 public function register(): void
 {
 //
 }

 /**
 * Bootstrap services.
 */
 public function boot(): void
 {
 //
 }
}

answer:
In Laravel 11, you no longer need to manually add your service providers in `config/app.php`. Instead, use the `ServiceProvider::addProviderToBootstrapFile` method within your package's service provider to automatically register it with the application. Here’s how you can do it:

```php
use Illuminate\Support\ServiceProvider;

class PaytmWalletServiceProvider extends ServiceProvider
{
    public function boot(): void
    {
        // Your boot logic here...
    }

    public function register(): void
    {
        // Your register logic here...

        // Automatically add the service provider to bootstrap file
        ServiceProvider::addProviderToBootstrapFile(static class);
    }
}
```

This method ensures your provider is registered in `bootstrap/providers.php` without needing manual edits. Make sure you have the correct namespace and class name for `PaytmWalletServiceProvider`.

To send a request correctly to cPanel UAPI from your Laravel 10 controller and create a new email, you need to properly structure your HTTP POST request with the required headers and parameters as per the cPanel documentation. Here's how you can modify your `test` function:

```php
public function test()
{
    $response = Http::withHeaders([
        'Authorization' => "cpanel holidays:XPA6V9NSZ5JGKXU5VE2X214U53WROFI0"
    ])->post('https://example.com:2083/execute/Email/add_pop', [
