}
```

This approach ensures that Laravel handles the path correctly according to your filesystem configuration. If you continue to face issues, check your `config/filesystems.php` for any misconfiguration related to the local driver or storage paths.

If the problem persists, consider logging the `$location` variable in the `LocalFilesystemAdapter::fileSize()` method to further diagnose where the path is going wrong.

In Angular 17's `@if` syntax, you can store the result of a function in a variable and use it directly within the same `@if` block by defining the variable outside the template. Here’s how you can achieve this:

First, define your component class with a method that returns the value:

```typescript
@Component({
  ...
})
class AppComponent {
  isServerRunning = true;

  checkServerStatus() {
    // Simulate server status check
    return this.isServerRunning ? 'running' : 'not running';
  }
}
```

Then, in your template, you can use the `@if` syntax to store and utilize the function result:

```html
@if (checkServerStatus() === 'running') {
  <p>The server is running</p>
} @else {
  <p>The server is not running</p>
}
```

This approach allows you to encapsulate logic within your template while leveraging the `@if` syntax effectively.

To detect changes in the required validator of the parent control from within your ControlValueAccessor implementation, you can listen for changes in the `NgControl.control` object. Specifically, you need to subscribe to the `valueChanges` and `statusChanges` observables provided by Angular's reactive forms API.

Here’s how you can do it:

```typescript
import { NgControl } from '@angular/forms';

constructor(private control: NgControl) {
  this.control.valueAccessor = this;

  // Listen for changes in the required validator status
  const controlSub = control.control.statusChanges.subscribe(status => {
    if (status === 'pristine' || status === 'invalid') {
      this.parentRequires = control.control.hasValidator(Validators.required);
    }
  });

  // Clean up subscription on component destruction
  this.ngOnDestroy(() => controlSub.unsubscribe());
}
```

This approach ensures that you dynamically update `parentRequires` whenever the required validator's state changes, reflecting any toggling of the `required` attribute by the parent.
